{ title: "301 inline name capture"
  doc: "eu-oaz72.182: distributing an inlinable lambda that refers to an outer
        name must not copy it under a nested block that rebinds that name.
        `f` is a destructuring lambda (tagged inlinable) whose body uses the
        top-level `k`. The inner block binds its own `k` and has an inlinable
        `h`, so the inliner re-closes that block's scope; a copy of `f` there
        would have its `k` captured by the inner `k` and give 6, not 4." }

k: 3

f([a, b]): a + k

inner: { k: 5 h([x]): x y: f[1, 2] }.y

` { target: :test }
test: {
  RESULT: [inner = 4] all-true? then(:PASS, :FAIL)
}
